{title2=Groups}

= i
{parent=1d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Suppose every <group commutator> belongs to $H$. For $h\in H$ and $g\in G$, the particular <group commutator> $h^{-1}g^{-1}hg$ belongs to $H$. Multiplying on the left by $h$ gives $g^{-1}hg\in H$. Thus $g^{-1}Hg\subseteq H$ for every $g$; applying the same inclusion to $g^{-1}$ gives the reverse inclusion. **Therefore $H$ is a <normal subgroup>.**

We can now form the <quotient group>. Its <group commutators> are
$$
(aH)^{-1}(bH)^{-1}(aH)(bH)=(a^{-1}b^{-1}ab)H=H.
$$
A <group commutator> is the <identity element> exactly when the two elements commute. Hence **$G/H$ is an <abelian group>**. Equivalently, the hypothesis says that $H$ contains the <commutator subgroup>, so the quotient factors through the <abelianization>.

= ii
{parent=1d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Conversely, assume $H$ is a <normal subgroup> and the <quotient group> $G/H$ is an <abelian group>. Commuting the two <cosets> $aH$ and $bH$ gives
$$
(a^{-1}b^{-1}ab)H=H,
$$
so every <group commutator> $a^{-1}b^{-1}ab$ belongs to $H$. This proves the converse and completes the equivalence.

For the <dihedral group> of order ten, choose a rotation $r$ and a reflection $s$ with $r^5=s^2=1$ and $srs=r^{-1}$. Its rotation <subgroup> $\langle r\rangle$ is a <normal subgroup>, and the quotient by it has order two, hence is an <abelian group>. Consequently the <commutator subgroup> is contained in $\langle r\rangle$. On the other hand,
$$
[r,s]=r^{-1}s^{-1}rs=r^{-2}.
$$
Since $r^{-2}$ generates the order-five <cyclic group> $\langle r\rangle$, the <commutator subgroup> is exactly $\langle r\rangle$. Every <abelian quotient> must kill this <subgroup>. There are only two <subgroups> containing it, since the remaining quotient has prime order. **The complete list, including the trivial quotient, is**
$$
\boxed{D_{10}/\langle r\rangle\cong C_2,\qquad D_{10}/D_{10}\cong\{1\}.}
$$
This is the order-ten instance of the <abelianization of an odd dihedral group>.

= 2D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= Solution
{parent=2D}

<Lagrange's theorem> states that, for a <finite group> $G$ and a <subgroup> $H$,
$$
\boxed{|G|=[G:H]|H|.}
$$
To prove it, define an equivalence relation by $x\sim y$ when $x^{-1}y\in H$. Its classes are the left <cosets> $xH$, which therefore partition $G$. Left multiplication by $x$ gives a <bijection> $H\to xH$, so every <coset> has $|H|$ elements. Counting this partition gives the formula; in particular $|H|$ divides $|G|$. Applying the theorem to the <cyclic subgroup> generated by an element shows that its <order of a group element> divides $|G|$.

Write the given order-two <normal subgroup> as $N=\{1,z\}$. Conjugation must preserve $N$ and cannot send its nonidentity element to the identity, so $gzg^{-1}=z$ for all $g\in G$. Thus $z$ is in the <center of a group>: a <normal subgroup of order two is central>.

Choose $g\notin N$. By <Lagrange's theorem>, its <order of a group element> is $2$, $p$ or $2p$. It cannot have order two: since $z$ is central, the four distinct elements $1,z,g,zg$ would form a <subgroup> of order four, whereas $4$ does not divide $2p$ for odd $p$. If $g$ has order $2p$, it already generates $G$. Otherwise $g$ has order $p$, commutes with $z$, and $(gz)^k=1$ implies $g^k=z^{-k}\in\langle g\rangle\cap N=\{1\}$. Hence both $p$ and $2$ divide $k$, so $gz$ has order $2p$. **In either case**
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_{2p}.}
$$

= 3C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= Solution
{parent=3C}

The <chain rule> along a smooth curve is
$$
\boxed{\frac{d}{dt}f(\mathbf X(t))=\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}(\mathbf X(t))X_i'(t)=\nabla f(\mathbf X(t))\cdot\mathbf X'(t).}
$$
Differentiating the circular parametrization gives the <tangent vector>
$$
\mathbf x'(t)=(-a\sin t,a\cos t)=(-y(t),x(t)).
$$
Thus the <chain rule> turns the angular derivative of $u$ into $-yu_x+xu_y$. The given <partial differential equation> makes this equal to $u$, so along every portion of the circle inside the upper half-plane, $U(t)=u(\mathbf x(t))$ satisfies the <ordinary differential equation> $U'=U$. Its solution is $U(t)=U(t_0)e^{t-t_0}$.

For the two specified points take $a=\sqrt2$. The initial point corresponds to $t_0=\pi/4$ and the terminal point to $t_1=3\pi/4$. The entire intervening arc lies in $y>0$, so no continuation outside the domain is used. **The requested value is**
$$
\boxed{u(-1,1)=10e^{\pi/2}.}
$$
These circular <characteristic curves> also show locally that the general solution has the polar-coordinate form $u(r,\theta)=C(r)e^\theta$, with $0<\theta<\pi$; there is no requirement of angular periodicity on the upper half-plane.

= 4C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= Solution
{parent=4C}

Let $R$ be an <orthogonal matrix> describing a Cartesian change of coordinates, with transformed <vector> components $v_i'=R_{ia}v_a$ and $w_j'=R_{jb}w_b$. Their <outer product> transforms as
$$
T_{ij}'=v_i'w_j'=R_{ia}R_{jb}T_{ab},
$$
which is the transformation law of a second-order Cartesian <tensor>. Here “rank two” counts tensor indices, not the <rank of a matrix>; a nonzero <outer product> has matrix rank one.

For an <isotropic second-rank tensor>, invariance means $RTR^T=T$ for every proper <rotation matrix>. The half-turn matrices $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1)$, $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,-1)$ and $\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,1)$ force all off-diagonal entries to vanish. Quarter-turns around coordinate axes then force the three diagonal entries to be equal. Conversely $RIR^T=I$, so **the most general second-rank <isotropic tensor> is**
$$
\boxed{T_{ij}=\lambda\delta_{ij},\qquad\lambda\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Since $vw^T$ has matrix rank at most one, it cannot equal a nonzero scalar multiple of the three-dimensional identity. Thus its isotropy forces $vw^T=0$. If $v\ne0$, a nonzero component $v_i$ makes the entire $i$th row vanish only when $w=0$; the converse is immediate. **The required choices are exactly**
$$
\boxed{v=0\quad\text{or}\quad w=0.}
$$
For the <Levi-Civita symbol>, multilinearity and antisymmetry of the <determinant> give
$$
R_{ia}R_{jb}R_{kc}\epsilon_{abc}=(\det R)\epsilon_{ijk}=\epsilon_{ijk}
$$
for every $R\in SO(3)$. This proves the <rotation invariance of the Levi-Civita symbol>, hence its third-rank isotropy. The convention is invariance under proper rotations: under an orthogonal reflection $\det R=-1$, the sign reverses. Thus it is a <pseudotensor> if transformations of both orientations are included.

